{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 10124 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "dadd55994fddddbb850bb8501bf19addaa9b03f58021f5de82e036756a262b2d" :edb/db-before-t 10123 :edb/db-after-t 10124 :edb/tx-data [[13194139543436 50 #inst "2026-10-28T18:36:40.564Z" 13194139543436 17592186576411 true] [17592186367729 1071 "If the solid region $R$ is given by \n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\hspace{0.167em}:\\hspace{0.167em}(x-3)^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 9,\\hspace{0.222em}y\\le 0,\\hspace{0.222em}y\\le z\\le 6\\},\n$$\n\nthen $$\n\\displaystyle {∭}_{R}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{z+1}}\\,\\text{d}V=\n$$" 13194139543436 17592186576411 false] [17592186367729 1071 "If the solid region $R$ is given by \n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\hspace{0.167em}:\\hspace{0.167em}(x-3)^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 9,\\hspace{0.222em}y\\le 0,\\hspace{0.222em}y\\le z\\le 6\\},\n$$\n\nthen $$\n\\displaystyle {∭}_{R}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{z+7}}\\,\\text{d}V=\n$$" 13194139543436 17592186576411 true] [17592186367729 1074 "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,z)\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/cc/ccc5cab37aed89c0d701ce0195414796211ddc73a498eaaa1ff145a45c42e9fd.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is one-half of a right vertical cylinder of radius $3$ whose axis passes through the point $(3,0)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\n\n- bounded below by $z=y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ and\n\n- bounded above by $z=6.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the $(x-3)^{2}+{y}^{2}=9$ in polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-3)^{2}+{y}^{2} & =9 \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta -3)^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta )^{2} & =3 \\\\ {r}^{2}{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta -6r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta +9+{r}^{2}{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta  & =9 \\\\ {r}^{2}({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta )-6r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta  & =0 \\\\ {r}^{2}-6r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta  & =0 \\\\ r(r-6\\operatorname{cos}\\theta ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire semicircle, we must have $r=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .$\n\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\n\\mathrm{Δ}=\\{(r,\\theta ,z)\\hspace{0.167em}:\\hspace{0.167em}0\\le r\\le 6\\operatorname{cos}\\theta ,\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi ,\\hspace{0.222em}r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta \\le z\\le 6\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{z+1}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{\\mathrm{Δ}}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{z+1}}\\cdot r\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathrm{d}z\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}r\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}6\\operatorname{cos}\\theta }}}{\\int }_{\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }}}^{6}\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}\\frac{r}{\\sqrt{z+1}}}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathrm{d}z\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139543436 17592186576411 false] [17592186367729 1074 "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,z)\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/cc/ccc5cab37aed89c0d701ce0195414796211ddc73a498eaaa1ff145a45c42e9fd.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is one-half of a right vertical cylinder of radius $3$ whose axis passes through the point $(3,0)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\n\n- bounded below by $z=y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ and\n\n- bounded above by $z=6.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the $(x-3)^{2}+{y}^{2}=9$ in polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-3)^{2}+{y}^{2} & =9 \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta -3)^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta )^{2} & =9 \\\\ {r}^{2}{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta -6r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta +9+{r}^{2}{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta  & =9 \\\\ {r}^{2}({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta )-6r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta  & =0 \\\\ {r}^{2}-6r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta  & =0 \\\\ r(r-6\\operatorname{cos}\\theta ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire semicircle, we must have $r=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .$\n\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\n\\mathrm{Δ}=\\{(r,\\theta ,z)\\hspace{0.167em}:\\hspace{0.167em}0\\le r\\le 6\\operatorname{cos}\\theta ,\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi ,\\hspace{0.222em}r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta \\le z\\le 6\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{z+7}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{\\mathrm{Δ}}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{z+7}}\\cdot r\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathrm{d}z\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}r\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}6\\operatorname{cos}\\theta }}}{\\int }_{\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }}}^{6}\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}\\frac{r}{\\sqrt{z+7}}}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathrm{d}z\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139543436 17592186576411 true] [17592186367731 1080 "{\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}6\\operatorname{cos}\\theta }}}{\\int }_{\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }}}^{6}\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}\\frac{r}{\\sqrt{z+1}}}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathrm{d}z\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta" 13194139543436 17592186576411 false] [17592186367731 1080 "{\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}6\\operatorname{cos}\\theta }}}{\\int }_{\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }}}^{6}\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}\\frac{r}{\\sqrt{z+7}}}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathrm{d}z\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta" 13194139543436 17592186576411 true] [17592186367732 1080 "{\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}6\\operatorname{cos}\\theta }}}{\\int }_{\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }}}^{6}\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{z+1}}}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathrm{d}z\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta" 13194139543436 17592186576411 false] [17592186367732 1080 "{\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}6\\operatorname{cos}\\theta }}}{\\int }_{\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }}}^{6}\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{z+7}}}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathrm{d}z\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta" 13194139543436 17592186576411 true] [17592186367733 1080 "{\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}-6\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }}}{\\int }_{\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }}}^{6}\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{z+1}}}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathrm{d}z\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta" 13194139543436 17592186576411 fa